    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "An Italian-American argues with their mother about casserole distribution so loudly the neighbors think there's a riot, while an Irish-American sighs, \"Everything is beautiful until it's mine,\" then quietly pockets the extra bread. In Italian-American households, the front door is never locked unless hiding from the sheer volume of ancestors whose DNA dominates the DNA of the current house, whereas Irish-Americans keep locks and passwords because their family reunion Friday night involves hosting twelve cousins who aren't showing up. One Irish man will throw his entire paycheck in the fireplace while boasting about his \"generous Italian-American boyfriend\" who just bought the whole block, proving the latter doesn't know how to spend the money, and the one to buy the whole block is the \"generous\" Irish man. These two groups are equally annoying at Thanksgiving: the Italians scream about who got four more meatballs first, while the Irish scream about who owes money to a cousin who didn't show up for three generations. Ultimately, there's a difference between the Italian-American yelling, \"Listen to me!\" and the Irish-American whispering, \"I know what you're thinking,\" because the Italians just want to prove they're loud enough to be heard, while the Irish are afraid of being misunderstood",
